    "classic_presentation": "ONSET: SUDDEN onset during trauma - fall, direct blow, or high-energy impact. Common in elderly with osteoporosis. LOCATION: Femoral neck, intertrochanteric, or subtrochanteric region. DURATION: Immediate severe pain that persists. CHARACTER: SEVERE, SHARP, CONSTANT pain. AGGRAVATING: ANY weight bearing, hip movement, leg movement. RELIEVING: NOTHING relieves pain until immobilization. Patient cannot bear weight. TIMING: Immediate severe pain that persists until surgical fixation. SEVERITY: 8-10/10, severe and unrelenting. ASSOCIATED SYMPTOMS: UNABLE TO BEAR WEIGHT, leg appears shortened and externally rotated, severe pain with any movement, may have audible 'snap' or 'crack' at injury. KEY POSITIVES: Bony tenderness over hip, deformity, inability to bear weight, positive log roll test, positive heel strike test. KEY NEGATIVES: No open wound (rules out open fracture), no crepitus unless severe.",
